 "amendments": {
  "items": {
   "": {
    "actClause": "AN ACT to amend the education law, in relation to authorizing personal tax refunds to be contributed to accounts under the college savings program",
    "basePrintNo": "S6942",
    "basePrintNoStr": "S6942-2015",
    "coSponsors": {
     "items": [],
     "size": 0
    },
    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Amd §695-e, Ed L",
    "lawSection": "Education Law",
    "memo": " BILL NUMBER:  S6942\n\n TITLE OF BILL :  An act to amend the education law, in relation to\nauthorizing personal tax refunds to be contributed to accounts under\nthe college savings program\n\n PURPOSE OR GENERAL IDEA OF BILL :\n\nExpands contribution options for individuals who choose to invest in\nhigher education by authorizing taxpayers to make direct deposit\ncontributions of personal income tax refunds into a New York State 529\nCollege Savings Program.\n\n SUMMARY OF SPECIFIC PROVISIONS :\n\nThis legislation amends subdivision 4 of section 695-e of the\neducation law to authorize taxpayers to directly deposit all or a\nportion of their personal income tax refund to the New York State\nCollege Choice Tuition Savings (529) Program.\n\nContributions must be a minimum of twenty five dollars and can be\napplied only for the tax year in which the refund was issued. Once a\ntaxpayer has elected to contribute a specified amount of funds to a\nNew York State 529 College Savings Program, the deposit cannot be\nchanged or revoked.\n\nTaxpayers may not contribute their tax refund to a New York State 529\nCollege Savings Program if their refund is reduced below the\ntwenty-five dollar minimum.\n\n JUSTIFICATION :\n\nUnder existing law, individuals wishing to invest funds into a New\nYork State 529 College Savings Program can deposit funds via\nelectronic bank transfer, check, payroll deduction (if available\nthrough an employer) or by a rollover from another college savings,\nvehicle. However, when New Yorkers receive their sometimes sizable\nstate income tax refund they cannot currently send that refund\ndirectly to a college savings account. Allowing taxpayers to directly\ndeposit funds from their income tax refunds into such accounts will\nincrease opportunities for taxpayers to invest in existing savings\nplans and help defray the ever increasing costs associated with higher\neducation.\n\n FISCAL IMPLICATIONS :\n\nNone.\n\n LEGISLATIVE HISTORY :\n\nThis is a new bill.\n\n EFFECTIVE DATE :\nThis act shall take effect January 1st.\n",
